    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-965",
      "problem": "Find all the values of $x$ that satisfy $12x+8 > 44$ or $5x+3 \\leq 28.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by solving $12x+8 > 44\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x+8 & >44 \\\\ 12x & >36 \\\\ x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/e9eec85ca2dd2a9fd01db97a5f281d16fd958e143e048f7ff3c5e7a87dd33f0a.png)\n\nThen, we solve $5x+3 \\leq 28\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & \\le 28 \\\\ 5x & \\le 25 \\\\ x & \\le 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/c9cec5cfc0e476fffe4f89f89ff0585992855e8e36fe09778029d5accd2bdc6b.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/dd1330c9f2d9dc2140a3e3a4dc37ac269d134c5e367f59c9985ce04d4acd6304.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $-\\infty < x < \\infty.$ We can write this using interval notation as $(-\\infty,\\infty).$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/observations/step-e965-1b828d1cf40d78133aec.html",
